A||||8||BC-GT||Anyone had problems removing oxygen sensor?||||1||
Z||000000||BC-GT||04-11-2001||10:23 PM||||I would like to replace my Oxygen sensor to see if I can get some better gas mileage, but I'm afraid the sensor will snap off or strip its threads. Do they come out fairly easy for the most part? Anyone had a nightmare experience doing it?<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||04-11-2001||10:28 PM||||Hey BC!<BR>I have never had a problem replacing one, but I had a cheapie fail...<BR>Just like pulling a spark plug...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||04-11-2001||10:44 PM||||I have replaced a bunch and never had a problem. The threads are coated with anti sieze and are easy to remove. gas milage will suffer since a bad O2 can cause the ECM to overcompensate. eg if the sensor reports a bogus lean condition the ECM will fatten up the Fuel To Air ratio when in reality the mixture it to rich.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||BC-GT||04-11-2001||10:50 PM||||Thanks guys, after pulling the PVC valve to check it and having the vacuum hose turn to dust I'm a little paranoid. Think I'm gonna get me some of those Rodney D. steel  vacuum line kits, seems  like the ticket.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jhar1987||04-12-2001||12:35 AM||||it's easy put the wrench on it and "SNAP" OOPS!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Galen||04-12-2001||01:53 AM||||Replaced the one in my 88 no prob... came right out, went right back in..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> sweet<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>Spinrpg.com - Free URL & e-mail Forwarding</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||04-12-2001||05:51 AM||||mines been gone for 3 years now, no ill effects. Maybe because an enriched mixture is good with turbo?||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||04-12-2001||09:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>mines been gone for 3 years now, no ill effects. Maybe because an enriched mixture is good with turbo?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes I'd imagine while force feeding air into the engine a fat mix is a good thing. What kind of turbo and power plant you got in that thing? I have often contimplated installing a turbo from a subaru I saw at a local used parts yard<P>||||reg||3
Z||000008||rogergarrison||04-12-2001||12:52 PM||||its a kit made specificly for Fiero V6. I bought it uninstalled from someone who changed there mind after buying it for $3000, got it for 1/2 price. Theres quite a bit more to it than a turbo and a tube. I wouldnt try to make a mickey mouse setup-find a complete kit or youll have problems.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
